[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Bit Manipulation and Mathematical Algorithms in Problem Solving"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Bit manipulation involves directly acting on bits, the fundamental units of data in a computer. Coupled with efficient mathematical algorithms, it is an indispensable skill for competitive programming and systems engineering. This tutorial explores fundamental techniques and their applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Bitwise Operators"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"AND (&): Returns 1 if both bits are 1. Commonly used for masking bits."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"OR (|): Returns 1 if at least one bit is 1. Used for setting bits."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"XOR (^): Returns 1 if the bits are different. Used for finding unique elements and toggling bits."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"NOT (~): Inverts all the bits."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Left Shift (<<) and Right Shift (>>): Shifts bits left or right, effectively multiplying or dividing by powers of 2."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Essential Bit Manipulation Tricks"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here are some common bit manipulation patterns that optimize time and space complexity."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"bool isPowerOfTwo(int n) {
    return n > 0 && (n & (n - 1)) == 0;
}"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"int countSetBits(int n) {
    int count = 0;
    while (n) {
        n &= (n - 1); // Clears the lowest set bit
        count++;
    }
    return count;
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Mathematical Algorithms: GCD and LCM"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Euclidean algorithm is an efficient method for computing the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two numbers, running in logarithmic time."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"int gcd(int a, int b) {
    while (b != 0) {
        int temp = b;
        b = a % b;
        a = temp;
    }
    return a;
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Sieve of Eratosthenes"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"To find all prime numbers up to a given limit efficiently, the Sieve of Eratosthenes is the standard algorithm, operating in O(n log log n) time."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"void sieve(int n, vector<bool>& isPrime) {
    isPrime.assign(n + 1, true);
    isPrime[0] = isPrime[1] = false;
    for (int p = 2; p * p <= n; p++) {
        if (isPrime[p]) {
            for (int i = p * p; i <= n; i += p)
                isPrime[i] = false;
        }
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Combining bit manipulation with number theory allows developers to solve complex computational problems within strict time limits. Practice these patterns to recognize optimization opportunities in algorithmic challenges."}]
